#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161666 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7e6407-bb34-5603-b325-580ac32f8a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a T-Score"}] :question/id #uuid "7898bd86-2b83-5cdd-af38-43e9ac9004e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10042" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X \\sim T_{9},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\geq x) = 0.005.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $1.397$ | $1.860$ | $2.306$ | $2.896$ | $3.355$ |\n| $9$ | $1.383$ | $1.833$ | $2.262$ | $2.821$ | $3.250$ |\n| $10$ | $1.372$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $2.764$ | $3.169$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X \\sim T_{9},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=9.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.005}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $1.397$ | $1.860$ | $2.306$ | $2.896$ | $3.355$ |\n| $9$ | $1.383$ | $1.833$ | $2.262$ | $2.821$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}3.250}$ |\n| $10$ | $1.372$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $2.764$ | $3.169$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq {\\color{red}3.250}) = {\\color{blue}0.005}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=3.250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161667 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82f2479b-d8f2-5042-a571-7ba4c713dad8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a T-Score Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "86f614ee-dea5-5083-b18b-711f3e2bd84f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10043" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X \\sim T_{11},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\leq x) = 0.99.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n| $13$ | $1.350$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X \\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.99 \\\\  & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim T_{11},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=11.$\n\n| $k\\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.010}$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}2.718}$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n| $13$ | $1.350$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\ge {\\color{red}2.718}) = {\\color{blue}0.010}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.718.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161669 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162103 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6665557-2c04-5082-ba56-28f9376f00e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a T-Score Using Symmetry"}] :question/id #uuid "1f694b6c-8ece-5e51-9618-5158e23521fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-10045" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X \\sim T_{13},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X| \\leq x) = 0.9.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n| $13$ | $1.350$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|X|>x) & =1-P(|X|\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.9 \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X| > x) = 0.1$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X > x) = 0.1 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nP(X > x) = 0.05.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim T_{13},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=13.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n| $13$ | $1.350$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}1.771}$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq {\\color{red}1.771}) = {\\color{blue}0.050}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.771.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7e6407-bb34-5603-b325-580ac32f8a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a T-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c224a8-7f3a-56f2-a4a3-ce3183720f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159399} {:answer/id #uuid "a15450a0-aadb-5532-97e5-2c2d3acc60c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.583" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159400} {:answer/id #uuid "da7d5d04-bfe9-5d47-81e6-692190f931bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/